<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Escher Wyss</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1805">1805</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Escher Wyss &amp; Cie</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Escher Wyss was a Swiss engineering company founded in Zurich in 1805 by Hans Caspar Escher and Salomon von Wyss, first as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a>-spinning mill. It became one of the leading Swiss machine builders of the nineteenth and twentieth centuries, producing water turbines, paddle steamers for Swiss lakes, locomotives and industrial machinery. It was taken over by Sulzer in 1969.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/escher-wyss.jpg" alt="Escher Wyss" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Roland zh · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Z%C3%BCrich_-_K%C3%A4ferberg_-_Escher_Wyss_IMG_2953.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
